# Glossary

## Git

A distributed version-control system used to track changes in files over time.

## Repository

A project tracked by Git. It contains files, history, branches and configuration.

## Commit

A snapshot of the project at a specific point in time.

## Branch

A movable line of development. Branches allow independent work on features, fixes or experiments.

## Merge

The operation of combining changes from one branch into another.

## Rebase

The operation of replaying commits on top of another branch to create a cleaner history.

## Remote

A copy of the repository hosted somewhere else, for example on GitHub or GitLab.

## Pull Request

A GitHub collaboration mechanism used to propose, review and merge changes.

## Merge Request

The GitLab equivalent of a Pull Request.

## CI

Continuous Integration. The practice of automatically checking code after changes are pushed.

## CD

Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment. The practice of automatically preparing or releasing software.

## Pipeline

A sequence of automated steps, such as install dependencies, run tests, build artifacts and deploy.

## Runner

The machine or environment that executes CI/CD jobs.

## Artifact

A generated output of a pipeline, such as a package, binary, report or container image.

## Container

A packaged runtime environment for an application and its dependencies.

## Docker

A platform used to build and run containers.

## Kubernetes

A container orchestration platform used to run and manage containerized applications.
